In France, elections have always been low-tech: voters hide in individual cabins and insert a pre-printed sheet of paper with a marked choice in an envelope.
When officials confirm the identity of the voter, they submit the envelope to the ballot box and subscribe to the electoral register.
France’s electoral legislative quite strictly determines this procedure.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent so that voters and observers can see that there is no envelope in them at the beginning of the elections and that no other envelope has been added, except for those submitted by properly enrolled and authorized voters.
