When a small group of living organisms (a small population) moves away from the main population from which it originates (for example, if it migrates across a mountain chain or a river or moves to a new island and it is difficult to return), it will often find itself in a different environment than it lived before.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so the new population will need different characteristics or adaptations in order to cope with them.
The original population has not changed at all, they still need the same adjustments as before.
When a new population begins to adapt to a new environment, over time it is less and less similar to the other population.
Over time, in thousands or even millions of years, the populations will seem so different that they cannot be considered the same species.
This process is called speciation, which means the formation of new species. Speciation is an unavoidable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
