The beginnings of Taiwan date back to the 15 th century, when European seafarers sailing past the island called it Ilha Formos or a beautiful island.
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in south-Western Taiwan and began the transformation of indigenous methods of cereal production and the hiring of Chinese workers to work on rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, the military forces of the Qing Dynasty (1644–1912) took control of Taiwan's west and north shores, proclaiming Taiwan in 1885.
In 1895, after his defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing dynasty government signed an agreement in Shimonoseca, with which it surrendered power over Taiwan to Japan, which reigned on the island until 1945.
