Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a wide range of ideas and practices, from a technological endeavor or a technological need to an idea that human destiny is driven by a fundamental logic associated with scientific laws and their realization in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism have the same general idea: that the development of technology itself follows a path that is strongly influenced by culture and politics, and that, as a result, technology causes "effects" on societies that are previously inherent than socially conditioned.
For example, it could be said that with the development of the car, roads will definitely develop.
However, the national road network cannot be economically justified for just a few cars, so new manufacturing methods are being developed to reduce the cost of car ownership.
Mass ownership of cars also leads to a greater incidence of accidents on the roads, which leads to the invention of new techniques in healthcare for the care of damaged bodies.
